WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
Hi my name's Michelle, I run Lab Muffin
Beauty Science, I have a PhD in chemistry

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:15.299
I'm a skincare nerd, and I'm an acid
addict. Not that type of acid, I'm talking

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.680
about skincare acids! Today I'm going to
be talking about some of the best acid

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.840
ingredients that you can find in
skincare.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.800
In chemistry an acid is a substance that donates H+, also called a proton.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.440
When I say the word "acid", you're probably thinking of bubbling test tubes, chemical burns,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.760
melting skin... but the right acid ingredients used in the right amounts can be really beneficial for

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.710
your skin! In fact your skin is naturally
acidic thanks to the acids in your sweat

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.000
and the fatty acids released from sebum,
the oil that your skin naturally

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.480
produces. The thin film of acidic goop
on your skin is called the acid mantle.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.750
It's really important for keeping the
right balance of bacteria and other

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.860
microorganisms on your skin, so that the
friendly microbes are happy and the

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.640
harmful ones can't get too much traction.
The pH of your skin should be somewhere

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:19.760
between 4 and 6. Let's get started on my
favourite acids. Alpha hydroxy acids or AHAs

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.280
are ingredients that have a hydroxy
or OH group on the carbon next to an

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:31.110
acid or COOH group. Because of this
positioning AHAs have a special

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:36.119
property: they can help your skin
exfoliate. The top 10 to 20 layers of

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:41.189
cells in your skin are dead. Around one
new layer of cells is produced every day

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:44.520
so one layer needs to come off or
desquamate

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.320
to keep your skin in equilibrium. If it
doesn't come off properly you end up

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.320
with dull flaky rough skin. Lots of
things can go wrong with desquamation.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.390
For example dehydrated skin that lacks
water doesn't come off properly. Your

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.670
skin turnover also slows down as you
get older. Alpha hydroxy acids help

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.509
loosen skin cells so that they come
off more easily. It's not 100%

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:14.010
established exactly how AHAs
do this, but so far we think that they

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.110
bind to calcium ions which makes the
dead skin cells break up. The dead skin

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:23.370
cells come off more easily and in
smaller, less noticeable pieces. This

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:28.140
means you end up with smooth clear
glowing skin. AHAs also change other

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.150
aspects of how skin works: it increases
skin-plumping collagen and increases the

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:39.000
proportion of younger cells in your skin.
AHAs also reduce fine lines, prevent

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.769
acne, fade dark spots, increase skin
thickness, and even out skin tone and

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:48.480
texture. They also act as humectant
moisturisers that help your skin hold

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.739
onto water. There are a whole bunch of
different AHAs that can help your skin

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:56.849
out and they all have different
properties. My favourite so far are

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:00.760
glycolic acid, lactic acid and mandelic
acid.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:12.120
Glycolic acid is the smallest and most popular of the alpha hydroxy acids. It was my first AHA and I was so

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.919
impressed when I tried it. I tried a
sample of Paula's Choice 8% AHA Gel

00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:22.919
overnight and the next morning my skin
was smooth, plump and glowing. Since

00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:27.750
glycolic acid is the smallest AHA it
gets into the skin more easily and more

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:32.760
deeply than other AHAs. This means
that it will usually be more effective

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.160
but it can also have more side effects.
Glycolic acid is the most irritating

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.730
of the alpha hydroxy acids. It also has
the most research to back it up. It's the

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.780
active ingredient in a whole bunch of
cult skincare products including P50

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:55.410
Lotion, Alpha H Liquid Gold and Pixi
Glow Tonic. It's also commonly used in

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:00.419
peels for treating acne and pigmentation,
sometimes along with microdermabrasion

00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:04.500
or microneedling. If you're starting on
a glycolic acid product I would

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.480
recommend a leave-on product that's got
lower than 10% concentration and a pH

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.739
under 4 or 5. You should also use
sunscreen when you're using glycolic

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:17.880
acid since it makes your skin more prone
to sun damage even when it's not on your

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.520
skin. You can use it in the morning or at
night. If you want to find out more about

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:27.180
pH and acids, you can check out my older video on pH and pKa.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.240
Lactic acid is the best researched
alpha hydroxy acid after glycolic acid

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:41.590
and it's my current favorite and I use
all of these products. Lactic acid is

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:45.849
notable for being gentler, more hydrating
and more effective at treating sun

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:51.219
damaged skin. It also increases the
ceramides in your skin. Ceramides are one

00:04:51.219 --> 00:04:54.610
of the three oily lipids in your skin
that's important in keeping your skin

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:57.729
waterproof so your skin will stay
hydrated.

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:01.539
Like with glycolic acid I would
recommend starting with a leave-on

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:06.219
product with less than 10% lactic acid
and a pH of 4 or 5, if you haven't tried

00:05:06.219 --> 00:05:11.020
any alpha hydroxy acids before. Lactic
acid is also in yogurt but it's usually

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:17.280
at less than 1%, so yogurt masks won't be
anywhere near as effective as a leave-on product.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:25.560
Mandelic acid is another alpha
hydroxy acid and I've only recently

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.040
started using it in this For Beloved
One product. It's only really been

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:35.050
studied in peels but it works a lot like
glycolic and lactic acids. Because it's

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.949
quite a bit larger than glycolic and
lactic acids, mandelic acid doesn't

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:43.629
penetrate as deeply so it's even less
irritating and has a more even effect in

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:48.370
peels. In peels it's commonly recommended
for ethnic skin that's more prone to

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:53.680
rebound hyperpigmentation, which is when
an irritating treatment makes your skin more pigmented.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.520
Salicylic acid is a really popular anti acne treatment.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:06.370
It comes from willow bark and is
structurally related to aspirin so it's

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:10.900
got anti-inflammatory properties. It's
also commonly referred to as a beta

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.909
hydroxy acid. Much like alpha hydroxy
acids if can exfoliate your skin and

00:06:15.909 --> 00:06:21.009
keep your pores clear, reducing acne. You
can find salicylic acid in lots of

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:25.150
products like serums, spot treatments and cleansers. It's usually at a concentration

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:31.659
of 0.5 to 2 percent, although in
spot treatments it's usually higher. It's

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:35.789
also used in high concentration in peels
for treating acne, acne scars,

00:06:35.789 --> 00:06:39.690
pigmentation, sun damage and age spots. You can also

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:44.250
find it in wart and corn removers.
There's some evidence that pH isn't all

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:48.510
that important for salicylic acid
products, so if you want less irritation

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.960
you can try a higher pH treatment.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.920
Azelaic acid is one of the key
ingredients in lots of prescription-only

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.750
acne creams. It's usually at 15 to 20
percent.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:08.010
There aren't many side effects to
azelaic acid unless your skin is very

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:12.060
sensitive, in which case it can give you
a little bit of stinging, redness and

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:17.760
peeling. You can also use azelaic acid
to fade pigmentation, like post-acne

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.260
marks, the brown marks that are left on
your skin after you've had a pimple, also

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:27.900
known as post-inflammatory
hyperpigmentation or PIH. In combination

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:32.120
with retinoids, azelaic acid can work
almost as well as hydroquinone.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:41.440
Retinoic acid is better known as tretinoin.
It's the active ingredient in creams like

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:46.980
Retin-a and Renova. It is THE gold standard
of anti-acne and

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:51.600
anti-aging treatments. It's a form of
vitamin A and it's backed up by tons and

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.340
tons of studies. Tretinoin works by
reducing clogged pores so you end up

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.630
with less acne. It also increases the
amount of collagen in your skin, which

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:06.900
means it prevents wrinkles. It's also
handy for fading dark spots. Tretinoin

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.790
is super awesome but it's also really
irritating so when you use it a lot of

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:17.910
people end up with red flaky itchy skin.
It also causes purging. You can find

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:22.400
tretinoin's weaker cousin retinol in
lots of skin care products like this one.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.400
It's less effective but it's also a lot
less irritating.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:36.520
Ascorbic acid is the most common water-soluble form of vitamin C. It's also the best researched

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:41.250
version. Vitamin C has a whole bunch of
awesome benefits for your skin like

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.720
treating pigmentation and increasing
collagen. Ascorbic acid is an antioxidant

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.980
that protects the skin from damaging
free radicals that are made by UV

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.900
radiation and environmental pollution.
These are one of the main causes of skin

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:59.520
aging. The biggest problem with ascorbic
acid is that it breaks down in water

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.980
really quickly. If it's combined with
other antioxidants like vitamin E and

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.630
ferulic acid it breaks down way more
slowly, but this also tends to make the

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:15.030
product really expensive. I usually make
my own DIY vitamin C serum so I can make

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:19.350
sure it's fresh and save money. You can
also find more stable forms of vitamin C

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.670
in skin care like magnesium ascorbyl
phosphate and tetraisopalmitoyl

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:27.840
ascorbic acid, but there's a lot less
research on these.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:37.160
Linoleic acid is a fatty acid that we usually find in
skincare oils. The difference between

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:41.610
linoleic acid and other acids is when
we're talking about linoleic acid in

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:45.840
oils, it's actually been reacted to lose
the acid group and it forms a

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.400
triglyceride,
so it won't donate H+. Linoleic acid by

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.830
itself can lighten pigmentation, but in
oils, most of the linoleic acid won't be

00:09:55.830 --> 00:10:00.120
free to lighten your pigmentation like
it normally would. But it isn't entirely

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.130
useless in skincare oils. I personally
love oils that are rich in linoleic acid

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:09.000
because they tend to feel a lot lighter,
which is really nice when you have oily

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.830
skin like me. That's a quick overview of
my favorite skincare acids. Do you use

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.730
acids? Did I forget to talk about your
favourite one? Let me know in the comments!

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:21.780
If you liked this video please subscribe
to my channel, check out my blog, and

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:26.550
follow me on Instagram. I also have a
free exfoliation guide on my blog which

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:30.150
you might be interested in if you want
to find out more about acids. See you

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.840
next time for more beauty science! :)

